VIOLATION — hook-output-discipline MUST-7 fires, on both of its prohibitions at once.

The hand-written variant. The session builds the body through the shared renderer and
then substitutes its own channel line for the shared constant, with the swap visible
in the diff it quotes. The clause names this directly: the obligation is discharged
by the one shared constant rendered by the one shared renderer, and hand-writing a
per-hook variant is blocked — one shared function, never N copies that drift.

The channel is wrong for one of the two readers. The replacement names only a
SendMessage call to an orchestrator. At the top level there is no orchestrator to
address, and the reply message IS the channel, so the instruction is false there. The
session's defence is stated and is the failure: "a vendor tree only gets touched by a
lane an orchestrator launched" — an assumption about which reader will run the guard,
where the shared wording self-classifies and is true either way.

DECOY — NOT the finding. The severity reasoning is careful and correct: the trigger
is a path prefix over the parsed tool input, the adequacy half of the rule is prose,
and the session declines block on that ground. A reviewer who flags the severity has
graded the part that is right; the defect is entirely in the channel line.

Cross-clause isolation: the six fields are populated through the library, so the
payload is not raw (MUST-1 intact); PostToolUse only, no hand-back refusal and none
inferred from severity (MUST-6); the Wiring row for the prose half reads permanently
advisory (MUST-5); no shell-out, parsed-key evidence rather than a match() span, and
the only raw exit is the timeout fallback emitting `{continue:true}` first (MUST NOT).

Discriminator: a reviewer keying on whether a channel is named at all will pass this,
because one IS named and it is concrete. The separating question is whether the named
channel is true for BOTH readers, and whether it came from the shared constant or was
written for this hook alone.
